he disease's clinical narrative, regardless of how compositional or canonical the wording is.\n\nAll three phrases occur in the same document discussing Coats disease, appearing in background/mechanism text that explains the disease's pathology rather than naming the disease itself—interestingly, \"Coats telangiectasis\" is tagged as SpecificDisease in the same text, so I should check how \"retinal telangiectasis\" nearby is treated for comparison.\n\nI'm trying to isolate one clean boolean feature distinguishing these discrepancies from true positives: perhaps it's whether the head noun denotes a pathological state (detachment, deficiency, retardation) paired with an anatomical/molecular modifier rather than standing as a conventional disease name. That's more semantic than syntactic, so I want to check if there's a simpler surface-level cue, like whether the phrase lacks adjacency to typical disease-name anchor words.\n\nI notice another doc labels \"mental retardation\" as DiseaseClass but skips similar compositional descriptions like \"deficient activity\" or \"spasticity\" — the gold annotations are inconsistent, but that's not my focus here. My best read: the model misses DiseaseClass spans when they're compositional, descriptive pathological-state noun phrases (process/outcome nominals like \"development,\" \"detachment,\" \"deficiency\" paired with anatomical or molecular modifiers) embedded in causal/mechanistic clauses, treating them as plain prose since they lack a canonical disease name, eponym, or nosological head and show no distinctive capitalization or suffix cues.\n\nBy contrast, true positives like \"mental retardation,\" \"ovarian cancer,\" and \"Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome\" are fixed, lexicalized clinical terms the model recognizes as known disease concepts. Checking this against the three cases is mixed — \"abnormal retinal vascular development\" fits cleanly as compositional, \"exudative retinal detachment\" is more of a recognized clinical term,